course: world
lesson: 68
title: Storms: thunder, lightning, hurricanes and tornadoes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A thunderstorm grows when warm, damp air rises very fast and cools, building a tall, dark tower of cloud called a cumulonimbus, which can reach higher than aeroplanes fly.
explain: Lightning is so hot, hotter than the surface of the Sun, that it can split trees and start fires, and it strikes the tallest and nearest paths to the ground, such as trees, towers and people standing in open fields.
explain: A hurricane is a giant spinning storm that forms over warm tropical seas: the warm water feeds it with rising damp air, so it grows stronger over the sea and weakens when it moves over land or cold water.
explain: In the middle of a hurricane is the eye, a calm area with light winds and often a clear sky, ringed by the strongest winds of all.
explain: A tornado is much smaller but can be even fiercer: a narrow spinning column of air that reaches down from a thunderstorm cloud to the ground and may destroy one street while leaving the next one untouched.
explain: For example, when a hurricane comes ashore, its winds push the sea up into a wall of water called a storm surge, and this flooding is often more dangerous than the wind itself.
fact: Thunderstorms grow in tall cumulonimbus clouds where warm, damp air rises fast.
fact: A lightning bolt is hotter than the surface of the Sun.
fact: Lightning can strike the same place more than once, and tall objects are struck most often.
fact: The safest place in a thunderstorm is inside a building or a car with a metal roof.
fact: Hurricanes form over warm tropical seas and get their energy from the warm water.
fact: The same kind of storm is called a hurricane in the Atlantic, a typhoon in the north-west Pacific, near the Philippines and Japan, and a cyclone in the Indian Ocean.
fact: A hurricane spins because the air rushing in towards its middle is turned by the spinning of the Earth.
fact: The eye in the middle of a hurricane is calm, and the strongest winds blow in the wall of cloud around it.
fact: A hurricane can be hundreds of kilometres wide and can last for more than a week.
fact: A tornado is a narrow spinning column of air that reaches from a thunderstorm cloud down to the ground.
fact: Most tornadoes last only minutes, but their winds can be faster than those of any hurricane.
fact: In a tornado the safest place is a small inside room or a basement on the lowest floor, away from windows.
fact: A storm surge is sea water pushed onto the coast by a hurricane's winds.
fact: Weather satellites let forecasters follow a hurricane for days, but a tornado can usually be warned of only minutes before.
exercise: What kind of cloud brings thunderstorms? | A tall, dark cumulonimbus cloud | A thin, high wisp of cloud | A flat grey layer of fog | Small white puffs on a sunny day
exercise: Where do hurricanes form? | Over warm tropical seas | Over cold polar oceans | Over large deserts | Over high mountain ranges
exercise: Why does a hurricane weaken when it moves over land? | It loses the warm sea water that feeds it | The trees and houses on land soak up all its rain | Mountains cut it up into lots of small pieces | The land pulls it down to the ground by gravity
exercise: What is the eye of a hurricane like? | Calm, with light winds and often clear sky | The windiest and wettest part of the storm | Full of lightning and loud thunder | A deep hole in the sea below the storm
exercise: What is a tornado? | A narrow spinning column of air from a storm cloud to the ground | A very wide storm that forms over the sea | A strong wind that blows in a straight line | A cloud of dust lifted by the desert sun
exercise: What is a typhoon? | A hurricane in the north-west Pacific Ocean | A small tornado that spins over a lake or a pond | A heavy snowstorm high up in the cold mountains | A great dust storm that blows across a hot desert
exercise: What is a storm surge? | Sea water pushed onto the coast by a hurricane | A sudden burst of very heavy rain that floods the streets | A gust of wind before a storm | A flash of lightning over the sea
exercise: How hot is a lightning bolt? | Hotter than the surface of the Sun | About as hot as boiling water | About as warm as a summer day | It is cold, like electricity in a wire
exercise: Which is usually struck by lightning most often? | Tall objects such as towers and trees | Low hollows in the ground | Places that have never been struck before | Lakes and puddles more than anything else
exercise: Where is it safest to be in a thunderstorm? | Inside a building or a metal-roofed car | Under a tall tree in the middle of a field | In an open boat on a lake | On top of a hill to watch
exercise: How long do most tornadoes last? | Only a few minutes | Several days in a row | About a week or more | A whole season of the year
exercise: Which storm is usually much wider? | A hurricane | A tornado | They are always the same size | Neither has any size
exercise: Why do hurricanes spin? | Air rushing in towards the middle is turned by the spinning Earth | The Moon pulls the clouds round in circles | The waves on the sea push the air round | Lightning inside the storm makes it turn
exercise: Why is it hard to warn people about a tornado days ahead? | Tornadoes form quickly and are small, so they appear only minutes before | Tornadoes happen only at night when no one watches | Tornadoes cannot be seen from satellites at all | Forecasters are not allowed to warn about them
test: The wind around a hurricane suddenly drops and the Sun comes out. Why should people stay indoors? | They are in the eye, and the fierce winds will soon return | The storm has ended and gone away for good, so it is safe to go out | The sea is now always calm and safe | The eye is the only part that brings floods
test: A hurricane crosses a warm sea and then a cold one. What is likely to happen? | It grows over the warm sea and weakens over the cold sea | It grows faster over the cold sea because cold air sinks down | It stays exactly the same everywhere | It turns into a tornado over the cold sea
test: Thunder is rumbling and you are playing football in an open field. What should you do? | Go into a building or a car as soon as you can | Shelter under the tallest tree nearby until it stops | Lie down flat in the middle of the field and wait | Keep playing until the rain starts, then run home
test: Which is true about lightning? | It can strike the same place many times | It never strikes the same place twice | It only strikes when it is raining hard | It only strikes in hot countries
test: A town by the sea is told a hurricane is coming. What is often the greatest danger? | Flooding from the storm surge | The bright flashes of lightning | The cold air after the storm | The loud noise of thunder
test: One house on a street is wrecked by a storm, while the house next door is hardly touched. What kind of storm was it probably? | A tornado | A hurricane | A snowstorm | A heat wave
test: The same kind of storm hits the Philippines and Florida. What are its usual names there? | A typhoon in the Philippines, a hurricane in Florida | A tornado in both places | A blizzard in the Philippines, a cyclone in Florida | A monsoon in both places
test: If the oceans near the equator became much colder, what would most likely happen? | Fewer and weaker hurricanes would form | More and stronger hurricanes would form | Hurricanes would move to the poles | Tornadoes would turn into hurricanes
test: A tornado warning is given. Where in a house is the safest place? | A small inside room or basement on the lowest floor | Next to a big window to watch it | On the top floor near the roof | Outside in the garden, as far away from the walls as you can get
test: thunderstorm and we're at the park, where do we go | Inside a building or a car with a metal roof | Under the tallest tree in the park, close to the trunk | Out in the middle of the open field, lying flat | Next to the metal fence at the edge of the field
test: how come the eye of a hurricane is all calm | The eye is a calm centre, and the strongest winds are in the wall around it | The storm has already ended when the eye arrives | The eye is where the wind is strongest of all | The eye is a gap where the storm sucks up the rain
test: Leo says lightning never strikes the same spot twice. true? | No, it can strike the same place many times, mostly tall things | Yes, once struck, a place is safe for good | Yes, but only if the place was a tall tree | No, lightning only ever strikes the ground once a year
test: a video said hurricanes and typhoons are totally different storms. right? | No, they are the same kind of storm with different names by region | Yes, typhoons only form over cold seas | Yes, hurricanes are just very big tornadoes that have moved over land | No, a typhoon is just a small thunderstorm
exam: Identify the safest place in which a person may remain during a thunderstorm. | Within a building or a car with a metal roof | Beneath a solitary tall tree | Upon an open hilltop | In the water of a lake
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the source of the energy of a hurricane? | Warm water of tropical seas | Cold air descending from the poles | Heat from volcanoes beneath the sea | Lightning within its clouds
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the eye of a hurricane. | It is calm, the strongest winds blowing in the surrounding wall of cloud | It contains the strongest winds of the entire storm | It is the point at which the tornado touches the ground | It is the location of the storm surge
exam: Ascertain the designation given in the north-west Pacific to the storm termed a hurricane in the Atlantic. | A typhoon | A cyclone | A tornado | A monsoon
